1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,717
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Intro to GPUs on Explorer. What a GPU is

2
00:00:04,737 --> 00:00:10,401
for, which ones Explorer has, and how to ask for one in a job, so the right work

3
00:00:10,421 --> 00:00:11,980
lands on the right hardware.

4
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:16,651
Ciao. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. A GPU is not a faster CPU.

5
00:00:16,671 --> 00:00:21,000
It's thousands of small, simple cores working at once, which is why it wins at some

6
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:25,078
jobs and loses at others. This lecture is the beginner's introduction to the GPUs on

7
00:00:25,098 --> 00:00:28,340
Northeastern's Explorer cluster: which partitions and card types there are, what a

8
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:33,233
GPU is, the three ways to get one, how to watch it work, and the two code habits

9
00:00:33,253 --> 00:00:34,320
that keep it busy.

10
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:38,913
Six terms. A core: one processing unit that runs instructions on its own. A GPU: a

11
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:43,171
chip with thousands of small cores for parallel work. A partition: a named group of

12
00:00:43,191 --> 00:00:46,862
cluster nodes with its own limits. GRES: generic resources, Slurm's name for the

13
00:00:46,882 --> 00:00:52,257
GPUs you ask for. s run: the command that gives you a live session on a node. And s

14
00:00:52,277 --> 00:00:55,380
batch: the command that submits a script to run without you.

15
00:00:55,450 --> 00:01:00,449
Start with where the GPUs are. Explorer groups its GPU nodes into partitions, and

16
00:01:00,469 --> 00:01:05,468
six are the ones you'll meet. gpu, the general one, up to eight hours.

17
00:01:05,488 --> 00:01:09,412
gpu-interactive, for Open OnDemand sessions, two hours. gpu-short, two hours too,

18
00:01:09,432 --> 00:01:14,073
but prioritised, so quick tests schedule fast. multigpu, for jobs that need several

19
00:01:14,093 --> 00:01:17,658
cards, twenty-four hours. courses-gpu, for classes, twenty-four hours. And sharing:

20
00:01:17,678 --> 00:01:21,960
GPUs that Northeastern PIs lend to everyone, one hour at a time.

21
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:27,493
Which cards live in a partition? Ask sinfo for the G R E S column. On the gpu

22
00:01:27,513 --> 00:01:32,367
partition the answer is a mix: V100s in two flavours, T4s, A100s in two node sizes,

23
00:01:32,387 --> 00:01:37,850
and H200s, eight to a node. The number after the card is how many sit in each node.

24
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:42,249
Before you submit, it pays to know what's free. sinfo with the node list, the GRES

25
00:01:42,269 --> 00:01:47,414
and the GRES in use shows that per node. And to check one node, name it. One trap: a

26
00:01:47,434 --> 00:01:51,492
node in the MIXED state isn't telling you about its GPUs. MIXED describes its CPUs.

27
00:01:51,512 --> 00:01:54,210
Only the GRES-used column says whether a card is free.

28
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:59,082
There's also a GPU Monitor app on Open OnDemand. It colours every GPU node as idle,

29
00:01:59,102 --> 00:02:03,905
mixed or allocated, and lists how many GPU jobs are waiting in the queue by card

30
00:02:03,925 --> 00:02:07,220
type, so you can pick a card with a shorter line.

31
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:12,981
So what is a GPU? Start with a core: one unit that runs instructions on its own. A

32
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:18,374
CPU has a few large, complex cores. A GPU has thousands of small, simple ones. For a

33
00:02:18,394 --> 00:02:22,815
problem you can split into thousands of identical little pieces, the GPU is the

34
00:02:22,835 --> 00:02:23,450
right tool.

35
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:27,872
Two pictures from the training. Delivering thousands of pizzas across a city: a few

36
00:02:27,892 --> 00:02:31,932
Ferraris, or thousands of bicycles? The bicycles. Or a room of professors at

37
00:02:31,952 --> 00:02:35,991
whiteboards against a stadium of students with calculators. The professors win at a

38
00:02:36,011 --> 00:02:40,988
hard proof. But to multiply a very large matrix, a multiply and an add for every

39
00:02:41,008 --> 00:02:44,111
cell, the stadium wins. Machine learning, scientific simulation, data analysis:

40
00:02:44,131 --> 00:02:45,360
those are stadium jobs.

41
00:02:45,430 --> 00:02:50,540
Three ways to use them. Open OnDemand, a website where a GPU session is a form. An

42
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:55,066
interactive session from the terminal with s run. And a batch job, a script you

43
00:02:55,086 --> 00:02:57,480
submit with s batch and walk away from.

44
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:02,655
One setup for all three. Log in, go to your scratch directory, and clone the

45
00:03:02,675 --> 00:03:06,755
training repository. Everything the demos use is in there: a Python environment

46
00:03:06,775 --> 00:03:09,830
script, a tiny GPU check, and the example code.

47
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:15,282
First way: the browser. On the Open OnDemand dashboard, open Standard Apps and pick

48
00:03:15,302 --> 00:03:16,440
VS Code Server.

49
00:03:16,510 --> 00:03:20,931
The form asks for a simple password for the editor, a partition, sharing here, and

50
00:03:20,951 --> 00:03:24,780
the number of GPUs, one. Leave the rest as it is and launch.

51
00:03:24,850 --> 00:03:29,601
When the session connects, open the cloned folder in VS Code, then a terminal from

52
00:03:29,621 --> 00:03:34,053
the View menu. Make the environment script executable, run it, and check that which

53
00:03:34,073 --> 00:03:38,187
python points into the new environment. Then the smallest possible test: the script

54
00:03:38,207 --> 00:03:42,640
prints the CUDA visible devices variable. Zero means the scheduler gave you a GPU.

55
00:03:42,710 --> 00:03:47,561
Second way: a live session from the terminal. s run on the sharing partition with

56
00:03:47,581 --> 00:03:52,757
one GPU drops you onto a GPU node. Activate the same environment and run the fuller

57
00:03:52,777 --> 00:03:55,030
check, which asks PyTorch for the card.

58
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:58,723
Here's that check. It prints the PyTorch version, whether CUDA is available, the

59
00:03:58,743 --> 00:04:03,486
GPU's name, and then puts a tiny tensor on the card and prints where it lives. Open

60
00:04:03,506 --> 00:04:09,090
it in v i, add an NVIDIA S M I line, and run it again to see the card itself.

61
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:13,869
Third way: batch. The same work as a script with S BATCH lines: the gpu partition,

62
00:04:13,889 --> 00:04:18,008
one GPU, four CPUs, sixteen gigabytes, one hour, and log files. It activates the

63
00:04:18,028 --> 00:04:23,033
environment and runs the check. Submit it with s batch; it runs when a card is free,

64
00:04:23,053 --> 00:04:26,580
writes its output to the logs folder, and you read it later.

65
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:31,999
Is the card actually working? Put NVIDIA S M I in your script to print its state

66
00:04:32,019 --> 00:04:37,052
while the job runs. And after a job finishes, gpu-logs with the job ID gives a

67
00:04:37,072 --> 00:04:40,842
minute-by-minute record. This real one from the training: for five minutes nothing.

68
00:04:40,862 --> 00:04:44,000
Then memory climbs to twenty-three percent, then seventy-eight, with utilization

69
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:48,422
still zero: the model is loading. Only at the end does utilization reach fifty-six

70
00:04:48,442 --> 00:04:51,580
percent. Most of this job's time, the GPU was waiting.

71
00:04:51,650 --> 00:04:56,503
Two habits keep a GPU busy. First, batching. The naive version sends fifty thousand

72
00:04:56,523 --> 00:05:02,072
single rows to the card, one request at a time, and pays the overhead fifty thousand

73
00:05:02,092 --> 00:05:02,420
times.

74
00:05:02,490 --> 00:05:07,565
The batched version sends all fifty thousand rows at once and runs the model once.

75
00:05:07,585 --> 00:05:12,320
Same arithmetic, far less overhead, and the card's thousands of cores all have work.

76
00:05:12,390 --> 00:05:16,903
Picture it. Fifty thousand tiny parcels through the door one at a time, the card

77
00:05:16,923 --> 00:05:20,530
idle between each. Or one crate, and every core busy at once.

78
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:25,512
Second, data loading. With zero workers the CPU prepares one batch at a time and the

79
00:05:25,532 --> 00:05:29,827
GPU waits for each. With workers, pinned memory and a non-blocking copy, the next

80
00:05:29,847 --> 00:05:34,451
batch is ready before the card finishes the current one. Batch your data. Give the

81
00:05:34,471 --> 00:05:36,300
loader workers. Keep the card working.

82
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:40,402
Four habits to leave with. Choose the right partition: gpu-short for a quick job,

83
00:05:40,422 --> 00:05:45,033
and s batch for anything long. Request only what you need, and check a finished job

84
00:05:45,053 --> 00:05:49,664
with seff to see how many CPUs it really used. Use the GPU-tuned libraries, CUDA and

85
00:05:49,684 --> 00:05:53,717
cuDNN, rather than plain code. And profile before you optimise. Stuck? Email r c

86
00:05:53,737 --> 00:05:57,190
help at northeastern dot e d u, or come to office hours.

87
00:05:57,260 --> 00:06:01,187
Let's recap with Claude. Six GPU partitions; gpu-short schedules fast, and sharing

88
00:06:01,207 --> 00:06:07,107
is lent by PIs. sinfo with the G R E S column shows the cards, and the GRES-used

89
00:06:07,127 --> 00:06:11,711
column, not MIXED, says what's free. A GPU is thousands of simple cores: the

90
00:06:11,731 --> 00:06:16,973
stadium, not the professors. Three ways in: VS Code on Open OnDemand, s run with one

91
00:06:16,993 --> 00:06:21,578
GPU, or an s batch script. gpu-logs shows the card's memory and utilization minute

92
00:06:21,598 --> 00:06:26,511
by minute. And batch your data and give the DataLoader workers, so the card stays

93
00:06:26,531 --> 00:06:26,840
busy.

94
00:06:26,910 --> 00:06:31,500
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Here is a PyTorch loop I run on a GPU: [paste

95
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:35,839
it]. First, tell me whether it processes samples one at a time or in batches, and

96
00:06:35,859 --> 00:06:39,635
rewrite it to batch if it doesn't. Second, write the DataLoader line with workers

97
00:06:39,655 --> 00:06:43,974
and pinned memory. Third, write me the Slurm srun command for a one-hour test on one

98
00:06:43,994 --> 00:06:48,312
GPU and the sbatch script for the real run. Then check two things yourself. Did it

99
00:06:48,332 --> 00:06:52,380
keep the arithmetic identical when it batched? And did it ask for only one GPU?

100
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:57,330
Intro to GPUs on Explorer. At Nik Bear Brown.
